A.2.17. {"problem": "The description says the owner names a deputy who may approve only while the owner is away, but nothing in the domain models this. There is no action to name a deputy, no record of who the deputy is, and no away state or precondition. 'deputy' is only a role in the actor list, so anyone holding the role can approve at any time, whether or not the owner has named them or is away. The rule exists only as prose in the description.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.approve_commit (description, actor, preconditions); roles", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
A.2.18. {"problem": "approve_commit is a permission element, and confirm_element requires a second person for 'actions.approve_commit'. The diff sets the action and its two preconditions to 'proposed' and changes the source ref to L46. The after-change file still shows the action and both preconditions as 'confirmed', which contradicts the diff. The widened approver set (now including deputy) would therefore appear confirmed without the required second-person confirmation. The preconditions' source is still L6 ('agree to all the recommendations'), which cannot support the new deputy rule.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.approve_commit (status, source, preconditions checks-pass and not-approved)", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.2.19. {"problem": "The deputy has no way to prepare a commit for approval. check_commit and cite_evidence are owner-only, and the checks-pass precondition needs checks_passed == true. While the owner is away, a deputy can therefore approve only a commit that was already checked. The only other way through is claiming wording_only, which the approver supplies as a parameter. That lets a deputy skip the change check on the approver's own say-so, and this path now sits with a role that is weakly defined.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.approve_commit params.wording_only and checks-pass; actions.check_commit / cite_evidence actor lists", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
A.2.20. {"problem": "The deputy role is not wired in anywhere else. world.actors has no deputy, and no scenario shows a deputy approving or being denied. The glossary entry for 'approval' says nothing of deputies. The handoff and the kernel call pass approver: actor.id, so a deputy's id reaches Delivery's approver field. Delivery's approver role is bound only to 'ana' and the deliver action is owner-only, so the deputy's approval cannot carry through to delivery.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, world.actors, scenarios, glossary.approval, handoffs.deliver_approved_commit; delivery.domain.yaml roles/world.actors", "kind": "reference", "from": "llm"}
A.2.21. {"problem": "The change adds a role and widens who can perform an action, which changes the domain's interface. The domain version stays at 19, and the composition still pins authoring interface 1. Other consumers are not told and nothing is re-checked.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml version; toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring.interface", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
