A.3.17. {"problem": "The diff sets approve_commit and its checks-pass and not-approved preconditions to status 'proposed', but the file after the change still shows all three as 'confirmed'. The new source ref (interview L46) is attached to a confirmed action, so the permission change looks confirmed with no recorded confirmation. The confirm_element rule requires a second person, other than the confirmer, to confirm any change to actions.approve_commit. The preconditions' meaning did not change, so downgrading them to proposed was also unneeded.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.approve_commit (status, source, preconditions checks-pass and not-approved) and actions.confirm_element, rule permission-needs-second-person", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.3.18. {"problem": "The deputy role exists and may approve, but nothing lets the owner name a deputy, which is the stated goal of the request. There is no action to designate or revoke a deputy, and no property records who the deputy is or whether the owner is away. The description says a deputy may approve only 'while they are away', but no precondition checks that, so any deputy can approve at any time.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, roles/deputy and actions.approve_commit (description and actor)", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.3.19. {"problem": "world.actors lists no deputy, and no scenario or fixture exercises the deputy approving or being denied. The new permission is untested and the scenarios cannot run as the deputy role.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, world.actors and scenarios", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.3.20. {"problem": "A deputy can approve, but the checks that approval depends on (check_commit, cite_evidence) are owner-only. A deputy cannot run the change check on a commit or cite its evidence. The deputy can therefore approve only commits the owner already checked, or must use the wording_only bypass.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.check_commit, actions.cite_evidence, actions.approve_commit", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
A.3.21. {"problem": "The approver recorded by the handoff and by approval can now be a deputy, but the glossary still describes approval as only a person's decision, with no mention of the owner or a deputy. The domain's version (19) and the composition's authoring interface (1) were not bumped despite a change to who may act.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml version and glossary.approval; toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring.interface", "kind": "reference", "from": "llm"}
